# Maintainer: Genesi OS Team
#
# genesi-store — where a Genesi desktop gets its looks.
#
# The measure was RyoStore: shelves (lockscreens, rices, themes, bar styles,
# fastfetch, plugins, bundles, decor), a card per item, one click to wear it.
# What Genesi does differently is the part underneath.
#
# ── An item is DATA ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every item in the catalogue is a name, a picture and a list of ACTIONS out
# of a vocabulary genesi-store implements: set a scheme, set a wallpaper,
# write this config file, switch the bar, ask pacman for a locker, point the
# login screen at a theme. There is no field an item can put a command in, and
# ci/store-test.py fails the build if one appears. A store that runs scripts
# from its catalogue is a root shell with a nice card around it -- and the day
# that catalogue comes down from the network rather than out of this package,
# it is a REMOTE root shell.
#
# ── Everything is reversible ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Each action returns its own inverse, and `apply` records it. Revert is
# therefore not "apply the previous thing", it is the undo of what was done --
# including the case that catches people out, where a config file did not
# exist before and putting it "back" means deleting it again. The old file is
# kept under ~/.local/share/genesi/store/backups either way.
#
# ── What it may touch ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Downloads land in ~/.local/share/genesi/store. Writes go to ~/.config,
# through genesi-center-set where there is a key for it (it validates), or as
# a whole file with a backup where there is not. Two things need root and both
# say so before they happen, through genesi-store-helper under pkexec: the
# login screen (SDDM lives in /etc and /usr/share) and installing a locker.
# The helper takes a NAME from a fixed list, never a path and never a command.
#
# ── The wallpapers ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# One MIT-licensed collection (D3Ext/aesthetic-wallpapers), so there is a
# single licence to honour, and every file is recorded with the size and
# SHA-256 it served at build time: build-catalog.py downloads each one to get
# them, and the store refuses a file that no longer matches. A dead link is a
# failed build here rather than a broken card on somebody's desktop.
#
# ── Why source=() is empty ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Everything is installed from ${startdir}, the way genesi-center does it.
# makepkg's source=() takes FILENAMES, not paths: given "catalog/catalog.json"
# it looks for a file called catalog.json in the build directory, does not
# find one, and stops with "catalog.json was not found in the build directory
# and is not a URL" -- which is exactly how this package failed its first
# build. ci/pkgbuild-syntax-test.py now refuses a source entry with a slash in
# it, so the next person meets the rule instead of the error.
pkgname=genesi-store
pkgver=0.1.0
# 2: the first version shipped looked wrong on a real machine, in four ways.
#
# No icon, because none was installed -- the launcher drew the "no icon"
# circle. There is one now, and it is the Genesi mark itself (the same outline
# and five veins as genesi-plymouth's logo and the hicolor icon), on a tile,
# with a small download badge so a menu full of Genesi apps is not six
# identical leaves. The leaf the app drew in its own rail was a shape I made
# up; it is the real mark now as well.
#
# The rail's hover labels were drawn UNDER the cards: the rail is an earlier
# sibling of the shelf, and no z on a child lifts it out of its parent's turn.
# The window draws that label itself now.
#
# The icons were Material Symbols, on the reasoning that every Genesi desktop
# has that font. A real machine did not find it, so the fallback -- a leaf --
# was drawn nine times down the rail, one per shelf. They are SVGs shipped in
# the package now, recoloured at draw time.
#
# And the cards were a thumbnail with three rows of form under it. A card is
# the picture now: the wallpaper's own thumbnail (480px, shipped, so a shelf
# is a shelf of pictures before anything is downloaded), with a small desktop
# drawn ON it in the item's real colours. The shelf is a mosaic -- a whole
# desktop takes four cells, a wallpaper two -- rather than a row of equal
# rectangles.
#
# New with it: a detail sheet that lists what applying something will change,
# read off the item's own actions, and the Genesi login screen (a real SDDM
# theme, in sddm/, installed but never switched on by itself).
#
# 3: thirty-one login screens that are not ours.
#
# Genesi's own greeter is plain on purpose, and what people actually want is
# the astronaut, the Catppuccin glass card, the caelestia lock screen that
# matches the desktop they are logging into. Those live on GitHub, so the
# store now downloads them -- which is a whole Qt application, unpacked into
# /usr/share by root, drawn before anybody has logged in. Everything new in
# this release is about that sentence being safe:
#
#   * The archives are pinned to a COMMIT and fetched as YOU. Root does no
#     networking.
#   * What is checked is a digest of the CONTENT, not of the tarball, because
#     GitHub builds those on demand and re-compressed them once already. The
#     helper computes it AFTER unpacking, on files it wrote itself, so the
#     answer cannot change between the check and the install.
#   * The helper still takes a NAME: a key into the greeters map in the
#     catalogue under /usr/share, which only root can write. Every URL, digest
#     and destination comes from there.
#   * Only plain files and directories are unpacked -- no symlinks, no paths
#     that climb out, a cap on what it may write.
#   * build-catalog.py reads each theme's own QML imports and records the Qt
#     modules it needs, so they are installed BEFORE the greeter is switched.
#     A theme importing QtQuick.VirtualKeyboard on a machine without it is a
#     black screen at boot, not a missing keyboard. A theme that turns out to
#     be Qt5 fails the build here instead.
#   * Every card carries a screenshot of the real thing, and the detail page
#     carries the way back -- the console and the one command -- where it can
#     be read BEFORE the choice rather than after it.
#
# Also: genesi/preview.png, rendered from the greeter's own QML by
# ci/sddm-shot.py, which is the Screenshot= its metadata always claimed; and
# SDDM's stock elarun/maldives/maya are off the shelf, because they shipped no
# picture and a grey card among thirty screenshots is exactly what pkgrel 2
# set out to stop.
#
# pkgrel 4: those screenshots are no longer IN this package. They belong to
# the people who made the themes, and some of what is inside them was never
# theirs to license onward -- the astronaut wallpapers are described by their
# own README as "copied from one user to another", and two are frames of a
# cartoon and of a commercial game. So a card names its picture and the store
# fetches it, from the same pinned commit the theme comes from, checked
# against a digest, cached in ~/.local/share and asked for only when the card
# is scrolled into view. Genesi now redistributes none of it. Plus a seventh
# theme, ADIOR-enigma/hypr-sddm, and the author's name on the card itself.
#
# pkgrel 5: the session-lock shelf never worked. The Lock button emits a
# logind signal; hyprlock is not a daemon and does not hear it; hypridle,
# which does, was never installed or started. Every card applied cleanly
# and changed nothing. The cards now install hypridle and write a user
# service that runs it. Also: the detail sheet shows the fetched
# screenshot (it was drawing the generic one), and a login card shows the
# archive's size instead of "0 KB".
#
# pkgrel 6: the login screens and the session locks are two shelves now --
# they are two programs at two moments, and only one can lock you out of the
# machine. Every shelf gains a "Como vem de fábrica" card, because Revert
# undoes one item and the person looking for this has been clicking for an
# hour. The bar cards are drawn from each preset's own module list, so the
# thin one is thin and the one with the clock on top has the clock on top --
# fifteen cards from one generic picture were fifteen identical cards. Same
# for fastfetch, which now draws its logo, plus two options that put a real
# picture there in sixel.
#
# pkgrel 7: thirty-five more login screens, from Darkkal44/qylock. That
# repository is 604 MB of tarball and the Minecraft theme inside it is 200 KB,
# so a theme can now be described FILE BY FILE instead: its own files, pinned
# to the same commit, each with its own sha256, fetched by the store as you
# and checked again by the helper as root before a byte is written. Nineteen
# of them have an MP4 for a background and no still image at all, so the cards
# use the author's own animated previews and the shelf moves.
#
# pkgrel 9, all of it from one round of testing on a real machine:
#   * the drop-in is zz-genesi-store.conf now. SDDM reads that directory
#     alphabetically and the LAST file wins, so "50-" lost to
#     "genesi-theme.conf" -- which Genesi installs itself -- and to
#     "kde_settings.conf". Every login screen ever picked in this store was
#     overruled by a file further down the alphabet.
#   * a downloaded picture keeps its extension. A file called "picture" with
#     no suffix is not an image as far as caelestia is concerned, so every
#     wallpaper failed to apply with an error from a program doing the right
#     thing.
#   * applying something now retires what it replaced, instead of both cards
#     saying "em uso" forever.
#   * an applied wallpaper is copied into <Pictures>/Wallpapers/Genesi Store,
#     so it shows up in caelestia's own list afterwards instead of being
#     reachable only through the store.
#   * the bar presets no longer set `width` and `spacing`, which are not
#     caelestia bar properties at all -- so the cards stop promising a
#     difference that could never appear -- and the factory preset states
#     every value rather than leaving them out and hoping.
#
# pkgrel 10:
#   * five themes could not be downloaded at all. The store built its
#     per-file URLs without escaping them, and one of these ships
#     "font/The Last Shuriken.ttf" -- a space is not legal in a request
#     line, so Python refused it and the theme died on its fourth file.
#     build-catalog escaped it when recording the digest and the store did
#     not when fetching, which is the kind of disagreement that only ever
#     shows up on somebody else's machine.
#   * the session lock crashed the shell, and the reason is that caelestia
#     HAS a lock of its own -- a WlSessionLock, the Wayland protocol exactly
#     one client may hold. Putting hyprlock in as the session lock on a
#     desktop that already has one is two programs reaching for the same
#     single-holder protocol. The locker is a choice now, and it defaults to
#     caelestia's own.
#   * thumbnails are 1280px instead of 480. A card is most of the window on
#     the mosaic shelf, and a 480px JPEG stretched four times looks exactly
#     as bad as it sounds.
pkgrel=11
pkgdesc='Genesi Store — themes, wallpapers, bars, lock screens and whole desktops, one click each'
arch=('any')
url='https://github.com/Genesi-OS/GenesiOS'
license=('AGPL-3.0-or-later')
# python-pyside6 for the window; the rest are what the ACTIONS reach for, and
# every one of them is already on a Genesi Hyprland install.
# qt6-svg because every icon in the window is an SVG (see Glyph.qml: a font
# that turned out not to be installed is why they are files), and sddm because
# the login screen this ships a theme for is the one it themes.
depends=('python' 'pyside6' 'polkit' 'qt6-svg')
optdepends=(
    'genesi-caelestia-cli: colour schemes and wallpapers'
    'genesi-caelestia-settings: the fifteen bar arrangements'
    'genesi-center: the settings writer the store applies keys through'
    'hyprlock: session lock screens (the store offers to install it)'
    'fastfetch: the terminal greeting the Fastfetch shelf configures'
    'sddm: the login screen the Genesi greeter replaces'
)
source=()
sha256sums=()

check() {
    # The two halves that run as scripts. A syntax error in either is a store
    # that opens and can do nothing, or a helper that fails under pkexec after
    # the password has been typed.
    python -c "import ast,io;ast.parse(io.open('${startdir}/genesi-store',encoding='utf-8').read())"
    python -c "import ast,io;ast.parse(io.open('${startdir}/genesi-store-helper',encoding='utf-8').read())"
    python -c "import ast,io;ast.parse(io.open('${startdir}/app/genesi_store_app.py',encoding='utf-8').read())"

    # ...and the catalogue, which is the thing this package actually ships.
    # Parsing is not enough: an item whose action the CLI does not implement
    # is a card that fails when somebody presses it, so the shelves, the ids
    # and the vocabulary are checked here, at build time, against the CLI in
    # this same directory.
    python - "${startdir}" <<'CHECKCATALOG'
import io, json, os, re, sys
root = sys.argv[1]
catalog = json.load(io.open(root + "/catalog/catalog.json", encoding="utf-8"))
cli = io.open(root + "/genesi-store", encoding="utf-8").read()
vocab = set(re.findall(r'^\s+"(\w+)": act_\w+,', cli, re.M))
sections = {s["id"] for s in catalog["sections"]}
items = catalog["items"]
if len(items) < 40:
    raise SystemExit("the catalogue has only %d items" % len(items))
seen = set()
for item in items:
    if item["id"] in seen:
        raise SystemExit("repeated id: " + item["id"])
    seen.add(item["id"])
    if item["section"] not in sections:
        raise SystemExit("%s is on a shelf that does not exist" % item["id"])
    for action in item.get("actions", []):
        if action["action"] not in vocab:
            raise SystemExit("%s asks for %r, which genesi-store cannot do"
                             % (item["id"], action["action"]))
# A login item names a theme DIRECTORY. Genesi's own has to be in this
# package; anything else has to be one SDDM itself ships. A name that is
# neither is a login screen that does not come up, and that is not a thing to
# find out by rebooting.
for item in items:
    for action in item.get("actions", []):
        if action.get("action") != "login":
            continue
        theme = action.get("theme")
        if theme == "genesi":
            if not os.path.isdir(os.path.join(root, "sddm")):
                raise SystemExit("the catalogue offers the Genesi login "
                                 "screen and this package does not ship it")
        elif theme not in ("breeze", "elarun", "maldives", "maya"):
            raise SystemExit("unknown login theme: %r" % theme)

# The downloaded login screens. These are the only items that fetch a whole
# application and run it as root before anybody has logged in, so what the
# catalogue claims about them is checked here as well as in
# ci/store-test.py -- this one runs on the machine doing the building, with
# the helper that will do the installing sitting next to it.
greeters = catalog.get("greeters") or {}
helper = io.open(root + "/genesi-store-helper", encoding="utf-8").read()
allowed = set(re.findall(r'"([a-z0-9-]+)",',
                         re.search(r"INSTALLABLE = \((.*?)\)", helper,
                                   re.S).group(1)))
for item in items:
    for action in item.get("actions", []):
        if action.get("action") != "greeter":
            continue
        spec = greeters.get(action.get("key"))
        if not spec:
            raise SystemExit("%s asks for the login screen %r, which the "
                             "catalogue does not describe"
                             % (item["id"], action.get("key")))
        conf = action.get("conf")
        if conf and conf not in spec.get("confs", []):
            raise SystemExit("%s asks for %s, which %s does not ship"
                             % (item["id"], conf, action["key"]))
for key, spec in greeters.items():
    if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{64}", spec.get("tree", "")):
        raise SystemExit("%s has no content digest, so nothing would check "
                         "what gets unpacked into /usr/share" % key)
    # Two shapes: a repository holding one theme arrives as an archive, and a
    # repository holding forty is described file by file (qylock is 604 MB of
    # tarball for a 200 KB login screen). Both have to be pinned to a commit,
    # and in the second case every file has to carry its own digest, because
    # there is no archive checksum standing in for them.
    if spec.get("files"):
        if not re.search(r"^https://[a-z0-9.]+/.*/[0-9a-f]{40}/",
                         spec.get("base", "")):
            raise SystemExit("%s is not pinned to a commit" % key)
        for entry in spec["files"]:
            path = entry.get("path", "")
            if not path or path.startswith("/") or ".." in path.split("/"):
                raise SystemExit("%s lists the path %r, which climbs out of "
                                 "the theme" % (key, path))
            if not re.fullmatch(r"[0-9a-f]{64}", entry.get("sha256", "")):
                raise SystemExit("%s has no checksum for %s" % (key, path))
    elif not re.search(r"^https://[a-z0-9.]+/.*/tar\.gz/[0-9a-f]{40}$",
                       spec.get("url", "")):
        raise SystemExit("%s is not pinned to a commit" % key)
    unknown = set(spec.get("needs") or []) - allowed
    if unknown:
        raise SystemExit("%s needs %s, which the helper will not install"
                         % (key, ", ".join(sorted(unknown))))

# Every picture the catalogue names has to be a file this package installs.
# A card whose preview is missing is the grey rectangle this shelf was rebuilt
# to stop being.
for item in items:
    thumb = (item.get("preview") or {}).get("thumb")
    if thumb and not os.path.isfile(os.path.join(root, "catalog", "thumbs",
                                                 thumb)):
        raise SystemExit("%s names the picture %s, which is not here"
                         % (item["id"], thumb))

# ...and a downloaded login screen names its picture instead of carrying it,
# with the checksum that makes fetching it safe. A card that names neither is
# a grey rectangle; a card that carries one is this package redistributing
# somebody else's screenshot.
for item in items:
    if not any(a.get("action") == "greeter" for a in item.get("actions", [])):
        continue
    shot = (item.get("preview") or {}).get("shot") or {}
    if len(shot.get("sha256", "")) != 64 or not shot.get("url", "").startswith("https://"):
        raise SystemExit("%s has no checksummed picture to fetch" % item["id"])
    if (item.get("preview") or {}).get("thumb"):
        raise SystemExit("%s ships a screenshot that is not ours" % item["id"])

print("catalogue: %d items, %d shelves, %d login screens, every action "
      "implemented" % (len(items), len(sections), len(greeters)))
CHECKCATALOG
}

package() {
    # The one command. With arguments it is the data plane; with none it opens
    # the window, because that is what a person means when they type it.
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-store" "${pkgdir}/usr/bin/genesi-store"

    # The privileged half, deliberately NOT on PATH: it is started by pkexec
    # from the store and by nothing else.
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/genesi-store-helper" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/lib/genesi-store/genesi-store-helper"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/org.genesi.store.policy" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/polkit-1/actions/org.genesi.store.policy"

    # The catalogue, and the script that built it -- shipped so the provenance
    # of every link travels with the data.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/catalog/catalog.json" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/genesi-store/catalog.json"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/build-catalog.py" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/genesi-store/build-catalog.py"

    # The window.
    install -Dm755 "${startdir}/app/genesi_store_app.py" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/genesi-store/app/genesi_store_app.py"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/app/Main.qml" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/genesi-store/app/Main.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/app/Tokens.qml" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/genesi-store/app/Tokens.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/app/qmldir" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/genesi-store/app/qmldir"
    for f in "${startdir}"/app/components/*.qml; do
        install -Dm644 "$f" \
            "${pkgdir}/usr/share/genesi-store/app/components/$(basename "$f")"
    done
    for f in "${startdir}"/app/icons/*.svg; do
        install -Dm644 "$f" \
            "${pkgdir}/usr/share/genesi-store/app/icons/$(basename "$f")"
    done

    # The pictures the shelves are made of. Shipped, not downloaded: a store
    # whose cards are grey rectangles until you press something is a list.
    for f in "${startdir}"/catalog/thumbs/*.jpg; do
        install -Dm644 "$f" \
            "${pkgdir}/usr/share/genesi-store/thumbs/$(basename "$f")"
    done

    # ── The login screen ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    #
    # A theme for SDDM, the greeter every Genesi Hyprland install gets.
    # Installing it changes NOTHING on its own: SDDM keeps using whatever
    # /etc/sddm.conf.d says until somebody picks this one in the store, which
    # is the only moment a password is asked for.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/sddm/Main.qml" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/sddm/themes/genesi/Main.qml"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/sddm/theme.conf" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/sddm/themes/genesi/theme.conf"
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/sddm/metadata.desktop" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/sddm/themes/genesi/metadata.desktop"
    # The Screenshot= its metadata has always declared. Rendered from this
    # very QML by ci/sddm-shot.py, so the picture on its card in the store is
    # the login screen this package installs rather than a drawing of it.
    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/sddm/preview.png" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/sddm/themes/genesi/preview.png"

    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/genesi-store.svg" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/icons/hicolor/scalable/apps/genesi-store.svg"

    install -Dm644 "${startdir}/org.genesi.store.desktop" \
        "${pkgdir}/usr/share/applications/org.genesi.store.desktop"
}
